at she was told  they weren't going kerbin the comedy  folk duo Garfunkel and Oates each voice  a villain in the movie with Riki  Lindhome is poison ivy and Kate Micucci  is Clayface and rounding out the Joker's  posse of major villains is Harley Quinn  voiced by Jenny slate who was also the  voice of bellwether in Zootopia and  Gidget in the secret life of pets the  Lego Batman movie also pays homage to an  enormous stack of villains from Batman's  rogues gallery some are more familiar  like penguin who turns up in a LEGO  version of his  car that we saw in Batman Returns  there's also Killer Croc who we saw last  in the Suicide Squad movie - exclaims I  did something when he switches on the  bomb and there's mr. freeze who has a  touch of Arnold Schwarzenegger from  Batman and Robert about him when he says  no you freeze Red Hood turns up in a  blink in your missing moment during a  fight scene the official Lego site  describes this Red Hood as a mysterious  supervillain and adds that rumor  suggests he may have a secret connection  to Batman or one of his arch villains in  the comics Red Hood is an alias that has  been used by different characters -  significant ones being the Joker  and Jason Todd the lego jason todd  version of Red Hood has already appeared  in the lego video games with a  drastically different look to this Red  Hood in the Lego Batman movie and the  helmet cape and bow tie worn by the Red  Hood that appears from the Lego Batman  movie suggests that this is the Joker  Red Hood rather than Jason Todd in the  comics the Joker version of Red Hood was  an alias that the Joker used for his  criminal activities before he became the  Joker other villains who appear  on-screen very briefly include Captain  Boomerang aka digger who get kicked in  the head by Batman in the movie in the  comics he's a criminal who uses  high-tech boomerangs and we last saw him  on the big screen played by Jai Courtney  in the Suicide Squad movie  he's also man-bats who first appeared in  the comics in 1970 as Kirk Langstrom a  scientist who accidentally turned  himself into a bat man that hasn't  appeared yet in a live-action Batman  film despite being considered for  various cancels Batman projects the  closest thing so far has been the bat  creature that attacked Bruce Wayne  during his nightmare in Batman V  Superman perhaps one of the darkest  villains in the Lego Batman movie  although frankly that's a tough call his  condiment King he first appeared in  Batman the Animated Series and has often  been used for comedic effect with an  emphasis on condiment inspired puns the  character was later introduced into the  comics under the name of Mitchell Mayo  King Tut is a Batman villain that was  initially created for the 1960s Batman  TV series I was later adapted into the  comic book King Tut aka William McElroy  was an Egyptologist at Yale who  transformed into a delusional villain  when he lost his mind  after being hit on the head during a  student riot  egghead who turns up in the Lego Batman  movie egging the bat-signal is another  villain who appeared in the 1960s Batman  TV series where he was played by Vincent  Price Helen Ross first appeared in  Batman issue 63 where he set himself up  as the opposite of Batman helping  Gotham's criminal underworld killer loss  also had a fake identity as a rich guy  called Cameron van Cleer  and similar to that money had a moss  'mobile as well as a moss signal that  criminals used to call him kite man aka  Charles Brown in the comics is said to  be partly inspired by the peanuts  character Charlie Brown who struggles to  fly a kite kite man first appeared in  1960 in Batman issue 133 and as you can  guess he used kite base weapons to fight  Batman gentlemen ghost is a supernatural  villain in the comics who is created  when the thief Jim Craddock was hanged  by the gunslinger Nighthawk polka-dot  man is a Batman villain who in the  comics can transform the colored dots on  his trick now 